Z||000005||terryk||06-02-2000||02:18 PM||||Why aren't you using the Northstar ECM?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||06-02-2000||03:02 PM||||First, because of the complexity of the N* ECM. The N* ECM talks to the ABS, traction control, instrument panel, and climate control computers, and will go to limp home mode if any of these has a fault. I don't know how it will respond if these computers are not present at all.<P>Second, because the LS1 electronics are more advanced. I have a '95 OBD I engine. I don't really care that LS1 electronics are OBD II, but I do want a system that uses a MAF sensor and stays closed loop at full throttle. I don't know if the Caddy stays closed loop at WOT or not, but I know it does not use a MAF sensor.<P>Third, tweaking options. In two or three years, I think there will be quite a few go-fast goodies for LS1 electronics. Even if these do not materialize, there will still be a darn sight more goodies for LS1 than for Northstar.||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||06-03-2000||01:53 AM||||Interesting perspective.||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||06-03-2000||01:00 PM||||Terry, I'm doing a swap using a '96,3.4TDC motor,and am wondering if you know of anything besides the shift solenoid circuit,VSS circuit,and Theft Deterent circuit that would need attention before the ECM will allow the engine to run proper?<BR>TIA||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||06-03-2000||03:19 PM||||Hi LowCG,<P>Run proper or just run?<P>I know the 91-94, but I haven't played with the 95 or 96 much. <P>Part of it depends on the transmission. Since you mentioned the SCS's, I'd assume you are doing a manual swap. It will possibly complain about:<P>1. SCS's.<BR>2. Gear switch (PRNDL)<BR>3. Manual clutch switch if they used it (shift anticapation and idle stability) <BR>4. VATS (if it's enabled).<BR>5. VSS (you will need a DRAC or speedo conversion box).<P>The 96 ECM/PCM is a flash box (no removable mem-cal) so the calibrations can't be changed easily. The ABS, HVAC, etc are generally benign in that them not being there usually doesn't affect anything. The ECM handles everything normally and is usually sent a message over the serial data link (from the HVAC, ABS, etc) to ask the ECM to do something unusual (limit torque, anticipate the A/C coming on and offset the idle, etc.), but if they don't send any messages, nothing bad happens. <P>On my 3800 SC Ciera, I've run a non-ODB and ODB/I ECM that interfaces with the ABS, HVAC, etc and they just listen to messages sent from the ECM. The ECM could care less if they are there. If the ECM receives a request from the ABS, it does something, otherwise it doesn't care. <P>It's possible the later ODBII stuff does send ping messages and the ECM listens for them and hates it when they don't get an "I'm here" message. <P>That's about all I can tell you. If you are not in an emissions test state or area, you might want to look into running the engine on speed density with a 92-93 ECM/map. I know a guy that has done that.<P>My experience is that fooling an ODB II ECM/PCM/BCM with resistors and the like tends to make for a less than optimal running engine. At the same time, I know the last of the DOHC's are very nice engines so I understand why you selected it. I have a 92/93 DOHC I'm going to use.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000010||Patrick's Dad||06-03-2000||08:04 PM||||Terry,<P>I recently had those same questions, but for two cylinders less. I've been seriously considering the 3.5 from the Olds cars (Aurora, Intrigue, etc.) instead of the 3.4. Aside from the physical issues (Motor mounts), it occurred to me that it may not be easy to dumb down an OBDII engine (Which was inherently designed as such) to OBDI (Which the 3.4 can be had in either flavor). Insights for someone who's not an EE?||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||06-04-2000||03:32 AM||||Hi Patrick's dad,<P>I'm not sure what you are asking. It's possible to do *enough* cheating on the ODB II ECM to get it to run well enough that no one would know much of a difference. <P>It depends on transmissions too. Auto's are easier to deal with. Manuals can make everything a mess.<P>Also, I'm biased in that here in California, we can't do hack jobs and get the swap certified (and that's not intended as an insult to anyone). That's why I started hacking ECM's. I've also learned what combinations work and which ones are very ugly to try.<P>3.4L DOHC are dead until 3000 RPM and then all he11 breaks loose. Great for road racing, but not for street racing. I don't know much about the 3.5L Shortstar. I imagine is somewhat the same in character. <P>I still think the Buick V6's are the best all around.<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000012||Jay||06-06-2000||04:27 PM||||Okay, My harness was fabricated by Howell engine disigns in Michigan.  They wired up my 1997 Northstar to run off of a 1995 ecm in order to bypass the OBD2.  Talk to a guy at Howell Engine Developments named Troy at 1-810-765-5100, he really knows his stuff!  Design One won't give you any info unless you buy from them, trust me I've tried!  The wiring from Howell was straight plug in and everything was labelled.  Only about 10 wires had to be spliced into the Fiero Harness.  Otherwise, I can't help you with the actuall sensor, but I'm sure Troy would know.<BR>Check me out at <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
